Lobe Icons
==========

This directory contains a vendored snapshot of eight brand mark SVGs from
the Lobe Icons project.

Repository: https://github.com/lobehub/lobe-icons
Commit:     4aaf4ee1fb2678a7f989ea570f0f6ce14a9abf75
Path:       packages/static-svg/icons/

Only these files are used, one mark per coding agent the status panel can
detect; the upstream repository is ~75 MB of React packages and raster
exports that ThinkTerm has no use for, which is why this is a copied
snapshot rather than a submodule.

    claude-color.svg    Claude Code    colored
    copilot-color.svg   Copilot CLI    colored
    kimi-color.svg      Kimi Code      colored, dark theme only
    kimi.svg            Kimi Code      monochrome, light theme
    openai.svg          Codex          monochrome
    cursor.svg          Cursor Agent   monochrome
    opencode.svg        OpenCode       monochrome
    pi.svg              Pi             monochrome

The marks come in two forms, because the brands do. The monochrome ones
carry `fill="currentColor"`, which `SvgIcon::rasterize` turns white so the
shader can tint them with the surrounding UI color — so they invert with
the theme for free. OpenAI, Cursor, OpenCode and Pi publish no colored
mark at all; their logos are monochrome by design.

The colored ones carry their own fills and gradients and are painted
as-is through `BrandIcon`, which skips its usual fill injection for them
(injecting onto a path that already has a fill is a hard usvg parse
error, not a cosmetic bug). Kimi is the awkward case: its colored mark is
a white glyph with a blue dot, which reads on dark chrome and all but
disappears on light, so both forms are vendored and the resolver picks by
appearance.

To update a mark, copy the file again from the path above at a newer commit
and record that commit here.

Each logo is the trademark of its respective owner. They appear here to
identify which agent is running in a pane; that is nominative use and
implies no affiliation with or endorsement by those owners.
